+# Keep pickup reminders on SMS
+
+For volunteers proposing a cheaper reminder channel or changing pickup reminders. Larder keeps SMS because an email
+trial increased missed pickups, leaving packed fresh food to be thrown away.
+
+## Context
+
+Larder tried email pickup reminders from April to June 2025. According to the provider's report cited in
+[issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), 34% of reminder emails were never opened. Missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of
+pantry orders. Each missed pickup leaves a packed bag, whose fresh food goes in the bin by closing time.
+
+The pantry lead reported that many members share one phone in the family and never check email, but see SMS.
+Larder switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025; missed pickups were back to 6% by August. In issue 52, a volunteer
+proposed email again to save the reported SMS cost of €40 per month. This was the third such proposal that year;
+the maintainer closed it with the decision to keep SMS.
+
+The options are judged by whether members see reminders and collect their pantry orders, the resulting food waste,
+and the monthly sending cost.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free to send, saving the reported €40 monthly SMS bill. Rejected because the trial left many reminders
+  unopened and coincided with missed pickups rising from 6% to 13%, wasting packed fresh food.
+- **SMS**: costs the reported €40 per month, but reaches members who do not check email. Chosen after missed pickups
+  returned to 6% following the switch back.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep pickup reminders on SMS. The trial and return to SMS support paying the sending cost to reduce missed pickups
+and food waste. [The reminder implementation](../../larder/reminders.py) selects SMS with `CHANNEL = "sms"`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Larder retains the SMS bill. The return to SMS was followed by missed pickups returning to their earlier 6% rate;
+it did not eliminate missed pickups. Any future proposal to switch channels needs evidence that members will see
+the reminders and that the saving will not repeat the trial's increase in missed pickups and food waste.
